        "anchor": "joukowski-map"
      },
      "note": "Conformal away from the pole and critical points; global use requires a suitable one-to-one domain."
    },
    {
      "source": "projection-operator",
      "target": "orthogonal-projection",
      "type": "generalizes",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "math/linear-algebra/Linear_Maps.md",
        "anchor": "projection-operator"
      },
      "note": "Every orthogonal projection is idempotent, but a general projection needs no inner product and may be oblique. In an orthonormal basis, orthogonal projection additionally requires a self-adjoint matrix."
    },
    {
      "source": "discrete-least-squares",
      "target": "orthogonal-projection",
      "type": "instance-of",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "numerical-methods/Function_Approximation_Numerical_Analysis.md",
        "anchor": "discrete-least-squares"
      },
      "note": "The residual is orthogonal to the sampled approximation space under the discrete weighted inner product. Coefficient uniqueness additionally requires independent design-matrix columns on the positively weighted nodes."
    },
    {
      "source": "discrete-least-squares",
      "target": "rank-nullity",
      "type": "derivation-uses",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "numerical-methods/Function_Approximation_Numerical_Analysis.md",
        "anchor": "polynomial-least-squares-uniqueness"
      },
      "note": "Coefficient uniqueness is injectivity of the sampling map. With a finite-dimensional coefficient space and positive weights on the retained nodes, rank–nullity turns the zero-kernel condition into full column rank; the fitted vector can remain unique even when coefficients are not."
    },
    {
      "source": "orthogonal-polynomial-construction",
      "target": "gram-schmidt-process",
      "type": "instance-of",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "numerical-methods/Function_Approximation_Numerical_Analysis.md",
        "anchor": "orthogonal-polynomial-construction"
      },
      "note": "The monomial basis is orthogonalized with the weighted function inner product; the input polynomials must remain linearly independent under that inner product."
    },
    {
      "source": "polynomial-interpolation-uniqueness",
      "target": "polynomial-root-bound",
      "type": "derivation-uses",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "numerical-methods/Interpolation_Numerical_Analysis.md",
        "anchor": "polynomial-interpolation-uniqueness"
      },
      "note": "The difference of two degree-at-most-n interpolants has n+1 distinct roots and must therefore be the zero polynomial."
    },
    {
      "source": "hermite-interpolation-uniqueness",
      "target": "root-multiplicity",
